From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Wed, 29 Jun 94 18:26:10 EDT
Subject: [#94-6-201] Scenarios: What happens when nanotechnology appears? 

An earlier version of this message apparently was bounced.  Sorry if you
get it twice.

Peter McCluskey says
> If AI is achieved in a way that
>produces very non-human thought patterns, it may be easy for humans and AI's
>to hate each other. This is one of the reasons that I don't like the classical
>symbol-processing approach to AI, and hope that the initial AI's are uploaded
>humans, or if that is impractical then a connectionist approach that closely
>mimics the way human intelligence evolved.

  There has been work done to try and integrate a connectionist approach and
a symbol-processing approach. In this work, the lowest levels of processing
are connectionist and the levels above are symbolic.  A student working with
Mike Dyer at UCLA did a thesis on this recently. The sharp demarcation
between the two styles of computing is blurred in this case.  It is at least
conceivable that humans also display both connectionist and symbolic
reasoning.

  Even if humans are ``directly uploaded'' I think they will rapidly become
magnitudes faster than humans and they will have direct access to massive
amounts of digital memory at enormous bandwidth. They will likely acquire
more powerful forms of self programming then are possible with old fashioned
wetware.  They will become qualitatively different creatures in my opinion
unless they deliberately limit themselves.  Much of there thought processes,
goals and plans will become opaque to humans.

Robin Hanson says

>Given human abilities for organized hate toward other humans only
>slightly different from them, I don't think the difference bweteen
>symbol-based and connectionist AI will matter.  I think the key is the
>degree to which AIs or uploads are integrated and mixed up with the
>ordinary human society.  If AIs/uploads are creatures everyone works
>closely with, have as friends and neighbors, and which share the same
>political and economic institutions, then there is much less danger of
>war.  If AIs/uploads are banished into space, as Morevac suggests, I
>see much more danger.   [See my discussion in the next Extropy issue.]

I agree that integration of uploaded beings would reduce the likelyhood of
war. Based on my comments above it will be hard.  Here is a crude analogy :
uploads and humans in the same society will be like humans and monkeys in
the same society. They are not equal participants. In fact the
``participation'' of monkeys in a human society with respect to power would
be illusory. I guess you reject this reasoning. I look forward to the next
Extropy.

               Gregory Sullivan

