From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Wed, 29 Jun 94 20:43:34 EDT
Subject: [#94-6-202] Nanotechnology and uncertainty

In an earlier post I claimed that nanotech will introduce major new
uncertainties especially into warfare.  I think Steve Witham understood what
I was trying to say and made some good comments.  Robin Hanson was quite
unconvinced.

It is a big topic. I will explore a small piece of the topic with 
this scenario:

Suppose nanotech has been achieved but human-level AI has not.  Several
different factions are trying to develop it.  Suppose one group makes a
breakthrough which allows human-level AI perhaps through uploading.  Drexler
in pages 77-81 of Engines of Creation discusses AI with nanotech.  He speaks
of brains which are a million fold faster than human brains. He speaks about
packing ten thousand cooperating minds in a building. He even tries to make
it plausible by discussing drain pipes, power usage, optical fibers etc.
Let us at least temporarily believe him.

Suppose one group makes the breakthrough one year in advance of other groups.
Suppose after development and testing they are able to build the building
one year in advance of others.  What could they do with a building of brains?
They could do 10,000,000,000 man years of work on projects they are
interested in.  Could that many man years be used to design a weapons system
to incapacitate opponents? Opponents will be using computer aided
development too.  But will it yield anything close to as many man years of
effort?

The factions in this situation will face major new uncertainties.  Each
faction will be uncertain about the success of other groups.  They will not
know what can be done with 10,000,000,000 man years of work on a project.

Complaints: 

1) Maybe Drexler is wrong.  AI can only be 10,000 times faster or
maybe it can be 10 million time faster.  Maybe you can only pack 1000 in a
building or maybe 100,000. This is part of the point. There is great
uncertainty.

2) Man years is a bad measure. Fred Brooks showed in the Mythical Man Month
that more man years does not always help a project. On the other hand, added
cognitive capability converts a chimp into a human.  Human years are quite
superior to chimp years in terms of research.  More uncertainty.

3) Maybe the delay between breakthroughs of different factions is smaller
then that given in the scenario. The development testing and ramp up of
technology allows competing factions to keep closer tabs on on another.
Maybe some how everything speeds up and there is no less uncertainty than in
the past relative to the new time scale.

If old fashioned biological humans are in control then I do not think they
will be speeding up there abilities substantially. In this case, the notion
in three above does not seem very plausible to me.

Even if humans are not in control consider this.  Many interlocking
technologies will all be changing along with weapons technology.

Prediction technology:  How accurately will entities be able to predict the
nature of future weapons systems and over what time horizon?

Detection and recognition technology:  How well will entities be able to
detect the deployment of opposition weapons systems?  If one detects some
anomalies that indicate the presence of a weapon can one figure out what it
is and what it does?

Stealth and deception technologies for weapons:  How well will weapons be
able to elude and/or fool the detection and recognition technology.

Diplomacy : How well and how quickly will potentially warring parties work
together to avert war.

I am unconvinced that these technologies will somehow move together so that
a stable balance is maintained and warring factions will face uncertainty
comparable to historical levels of uncertainty.

Here is a fun analogy: A faction which falls behind will be like an ant
trying to predict insecticide technology and then trying to accurately
predict the chemical composition of the pesticides that will be used against
it. 


